                <pb facs="0678" n="671" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg671"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/11064: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-11-10T14:00:00+08:00">November 10, 1937—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:09 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>902. My 885 [<hi rendition="#italic">890</hi>], November 5, 11 a.m.
                    Representative of American Aviation Company states Chinese Government has asked
                    for and <pb facs="0679" n="672" xml:id="pg672"/> is accepting tenders for
                    pursuit and training planes costing approximately $3,000,000, $2,000,000,
                    respectively and spare parts and engines $1,000,000 each American currency. I
                    understand tenders submitted provide for cash payment and delivery ex-warehouse
                    in the United States. These purchases if made will indicate that the Chinese
                    Army has intention of resisting indefinitely.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, Peiping, Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
